      "problem": "If $\\left\\Vert {- \\dfrac{ \\mathbf{x} }{2} } \\right\\Vert = 5,$ find $\\Vert 3 \\mathbf{x} \\Vert.$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\Vert k \\mathbf{x} \\Vert= \\vert k \\vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we can solve for the norm $\\Vert x \\Vert$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-\\frac{x}{2}‖ & =5 \\\\ ‖-\\frac{1}{2}x‖ & =5 \\\\ |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\cdot ‖x‖ & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ‖x‖ & =5 \\\\ ‖x‖ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $\\Vert 3 \\mathbf{x} \\Vert \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖3x‖ & =3\\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\  & =3\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$",
